{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Because you have done this thing, etc.</b> Since it says ״וְלֹא חָשַׂכְתָּ אֶת בִּנְךָ״ — infer from this that the statement ״כִּי עָשִׂיתָ״ is a different matter. And furthermore, for above it said ״וְלֹא חָשַׂכְתָּ אֶת בִּנְךָ אֶת יְחִידְךָ מִמֶּנִּי״ (Genesis 22:12), and here it did not mention ״מִמֶּנִּי.״ And furthermore, the doubled expression ״כִּי בָרֵךְ אֲבָרֶכְךָ״ requires explanation, for according to Rashi's explanation — one for the father and one for the son — the doubling of ״הַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה״ is difficult. Therefore it appears to me: since in every offering he said 'May it be Your will that it be as if I had slaughtered my son,' etc. — behold, it is as if he did two deeds, for the offering of the ram he did in actual deed, and the offering of his son in thought. Therefore it says ״יַעַן כִּי עָשִׂיתָ הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה״ — that is, the act of bringing the offering, for in the merit of the sacrifices all the promises were said to him. ״וְלֹא חָשַׂכְתָּ אֶת בִּנְךָ״ — that is, from mentioning him in each and every offering. Therefore ״מִמֶּנִּי״ was not said, which would indicate actual sacrifice, for now he did not intend to offer him in actuality. Therefore ״בָּרֵךְ אֲבָרֶכְךָ״ — a doubled blessing, one corresponding to the deed and one corresponding to the thought, and this is the reason for ״וְהַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase יְהִי רָצוֹן כְּאִלּוּ בְּנִי שָׁחוּט refers to a liturgical formula traditionally recited by Abraham after the Akedah in every subsequent sacrifice; the כוּ׳ indicates the Kli Yakar is abbreviating a longer known passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:17.428313043Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}